     There are quite a few variants of LZDCMP floating around, and some
of them have a bug which prevents the final block of the file from being
processed correctly.  However, you should try ``lzdcmp -b infile outfile''
before giving up on it.  -b forces some sort of Unix-compatibility mode,
which may or may be used correctly if you let it default.  (You need to
create a foreign command first, by making a symbol whose value begins
with a dollar sign:  LZDCMP :== $some_disk:[some_directory]LZDCMP
substitute the appropriate disk and directory values.)

     If the lzdcmp from wuarchive won't work for you, you might try the
vmsnet.sources archives at black.cerritos.edu or acfcluster.nyu.edu.
wuarchive should also have a port of `compress' among the VMS tools, and
its decompress mode ought to work OK, although it doesn't support any
of the VMS-specific features available within lzcomp & lzdcmp.

